      "writtenOn": "2022-01-04T22:44:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hey David, guess I am looking thoroughly w/ fresh eyes after a vacation. Sorry, happy to re-approve.\n\nWhat does this allow, that would have been impossible before, by calling IIdentityCredential multiple times?\n\nThe way I understand it is that IPresentationSession gives methods which are currently on IIdentityCredential which are now lifted, and can be used to create multiple credentials (presumably, this reduces the amount of work that needs to be done, since this flow only needs to be done).\n\nHowever, I\u0027m curious if you considered something like IIdentityCredential::duplicate (a method that will copy the object at any point). This would avoid needing to have multiple implementations of these methods.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 265,
        "startChar": 25,
        "endLine": 265,
        "endChar": 50
      },
      "revId": "f05f5db007ea4b801e8d1585d140490c205d536c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ee24000a_09a6b2e7",
        "filename": "identity/aidl/android/hardware/identity/IIdentityCredentialStore.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 265,
      "author": {
        "id": 1076971
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-01-10T20:13:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hi, thanks for looking!\n\nIPresentationSession is a way to present multiple IIdentityCredential objects using the same session encryption ephemeral key and same session transcript. It\u0027s not to reduce work, it\u0027s to make sure they\u0027re using the same keys / transcript.\n\nI don\u0027t think IIdentityCredential::duplicate would work ... it would make the implementers life significantly harder as they\u0027d need to then support having two (or more) IIdentityCredential objects alive at the same time. This would double (or more) the amount of RAM needed in the Secure Area backing the HAL.\n\nToday we allow implementations which effectively only supports a single instance of IIdentityCredential ... only the latest created instance will have to work, it\u0027s fine for the HAL to return failures on previously created instances. If you\u0027re familiar with KeyMaster/KeyMint this is analogous to the \"operation slot\" constraints.",
      "parentUuid": "5705717d_2c088444",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 265,
        "startChar": 25,
        "endLine": 265,
        "endChar": 50
      },
      "revId": "f05f5db007ea4b801e8d1585d140490c205d536c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
